alertChannels:
  - name: Engineering Slack
    config:
      channelType: slack
      webhookUrl: ${SLACK_WEBHOOK_URL}
monitors:
  - name: API health
    type: HTTP
    config:
      url: https://api.acme.dev/health
      method: GET
    frequencySeconds: 300
    regions: [us-east, eu-west]
    alertChannels: [Engineering Slack]
    assertions:
      - config: { type: status_code, expected: "200", operator: equals }
        severity: fail
      - config: { type: response_time, thresholdMs: 800 }
        severity: warn Terminal $ devhelm plan -f devhelm.yml
$ devhelm deploy -f devhelm.yml WHY MONITORING DRIFTS Your app is in git. Your monitors are in someone's browser tab.  The monitor nobody can diff is the one that is wrong when you need it. A threshold changed in a form has no pull request, no author, and no way to keep staging and production on the same definition. The same monitor in the repo is a diff someone approved. Before In a dashboard No review before the threshold changes No history of who changed it Staging and production drift apart After In your repo Pull-request review Git blame on the line that pages you One definition per environment Dashboard edits show up as drift in devhelm plan Start free — no credit card CLI AND GITHUB ACTION From commit to monitor in three commands  Run them in your terminal, or let the GitHub Action run them on every pull request. Validate and plan on the pull request, deploy on merge. The file covers HTTP, DNS, TCP, ICMP, heartbeat and MCP server checks, with assertions on status code, response time, body or JSON, SSL expiry and DNS records. 1 Init devhelm init scaffolds devhelm.yml in the repo. devhelm validate devhelm.yml checks it offline against the schema. 2 Plan devhelm plan -f devhelm.yml lists what will be created, updated or deleted. Nothing changes yet. 3 Deploy devhelm deploy -f devhelm.yml applies the plan. In CI, add --yes to skip the prompt. Read the CLI docs Monitoring-as-code guide Terminal GitHub Action $ npm install -g devhelm
$ devhelm init
$ devhelm validate devhelm.yml
$ devhelm plan -f devhelm.yml
$ devhelm deploy -f devhelm.yml name: Monitors
on:
  pull_request:
    paths: ['devhelm.yml']
  push:
    branches: [main]
    paths: ['devhelm.yml']

jobs:
  monitors:
    runs-on: ubuntu-latest
    steps:
      - uses: actions/checkout@v4
      - uses: devhelmhq/setup-devhelm@v1
        with:
          api-token: ${{ secrets.DEVHELM_API_TOKEN }}
      - run: devhelm validate devhelm.yml
      - run: devhelm plan -f devhelm.yml
      - if: github.ref == 'refs/heads/main'
        run: devhelm deploy -f devhelm.yml --yes API health production · HTTP GET https://api.acme.dev/health Availability 100.000% Avg Latency 218ms P50 180ms P95 340ms Incidents 0 Checks 1,440 24h ago 100.00 % uptime Now Latest check All assertions passed Healthy HTTP 200 · 218 ms status_code equals 200 · fail ✓ passed response_time < 800 ms · warn ✓ passed Monitors · Example workspace TERRAFORM PROVIDER · BETA Your infrastructure is in Terraform. Put the monitors there too.  Review the plan before it pages anyone. devhelmhq/devhelm on the Terraform Registry. Monitors, alert channels, notification policies, status pages and their components, dependencies, resource groups, environments and secrets are all resources. terraform plan shows the change. terraform apply ships it. Read the Terraform provider docs Monitor Alert channel Status page terraform {
  required_providers {
    devhelm = {
      source  = "devhelmhq/devhelm"
      version = "0.2.0-beta.9"
    }
  }
}

resource "devhelm_monitor" "api" {
  name              = "API health"
  type              = "HTTP"
  frequency_seconds = 300
  regions           = ["us-east", "eu-west"]

  config = jsonencode({
    url    = "https://api.acme.dev/health"
    method = "GET"
  })

  assertions {
    type   = "status_code"
    config = jsonencode({ expected = "200", operator = "equals" })
  }

  alert_channel_ids = [devhelm_alert_channel.slack.id]
} resource "devhelm_alert_channel" "slack" {
  name         = "Engineering Slack"
  channel_type = "slack"
  webhook_url  = var.slack_webhook_url
} resource "devhelm_status_page" "public" {
  name        = "Acme"
  slug        = "acme"
  description = "Live status of Acme services."
}

resource "devhelm_status_page_component" "api" {
  status_page_id = devhelm_status_page.public.id
  name           = "API"
  type           = "MONITOR"
  monitor_id     = devhelm_monitor.api.id
} MCP SERVER Your agent reads the incident.  You still merge the change. Claude Code or Cursor can create monitors, adjust them and pull incident timelines while it writes the feature. Connect to the hosted server at https://mcp.devhelm.io/mcp with a bearer token, or run it locally with uvx devhelm-mcp-server . For changes you want reviewed, have the agent edit devhelm.yml and merge the pull request yourself. About 128 tools across monitors, incidents, alert channels, status pages and dependencies create_monitor , update_monitor , list_incidents , get_incident_timeline , track_dependency , get_service_live_status Monitoring your own MCP server is a separate check type, on every plan Set up the MCP server mcp.json {
  "mcpServers": {
    "devhelm": {
      "command": "uvx",
      "args": ["devhelm-mcp-server"],
      "env": { "DEVHELM_API_TOKEN": "dh_live_xxxxxxxx" }
    }
  }
} Example conversation You: Why did API health page us last night? Agent → list_incidents, get_incident_timeline Agent: Three failed checks from eu-west, then recovery. Here is the timeline. WHAT SHIPS Five developer surfaces.  All of them on Free. Commercial use allowed, no credit card. Paid plans raise limits: more monitors, faster intervals, higher API rates. They don't unlock any developer surface. Everything the dashboard does is a REST API call. Rate limits are per organization: 100 requests a minute on Free, 1,000 on Starter, 5,000 or more on Pro and above. Alerts go to 20 integrations on every plan, including Slack, PagerDuty, Opsgenie, Microsoft Teams and webhooks. CLI npm install -g devhelm Terraform provider (beta) devhelmhq/devhelm SDKs TypeScript @devhelm/sdk on npm, Python devhelm on PyPI MCP server https://mcp.devhelm.io/mcp or uvx devhelm-mcp-server GitHub Action devhelmhq/setup-devhelm@v1 Open the API reference See pricing FAQ Frequently asked questions  Plan gating, drift, rollback and agent access. Is monitoring as code available on the Free plan? + Yes. The CLI, Terraform provider (beta), SDKs, MCP server and GitHub Action are on every plan, including Free, with commercial use allowed and no credit card. CLI or Terraform, which should I use? + Use the CLI when the monitor file should travel with the app repo. Use the Terraform provider when the environment is already a Terraform plan. Both are on every plan. Can the dashboard and my repo both edit a monitor? + Yes, but the file wins. devhelm plan shows dashboard edits as drift, and the next devhelm deploy overwrites them with the YAML definition. How do I roll back? + Revert the commit that changed devhelm.yml and deploy again. Run devhelm plan -f devhelm.yml first to see exactly what the revert changes. Which SDK languages are there? + TypeScript ( @devhelm/sdk ) and Python ( devhelm ). Anything else can call the REST API. Does it work with Pulumi? + Not today. Use the Terraform provider or the REST API. Can an agent change my monitors safely? + It can change them directly, within the token you give it: the MCP server acts on your workspace through the API, and changes apply at once. If you want a review step, keep monitors in devhelm.yml and have the agent open a pull request instead. Book a demo Ship the monitor in the same PR as the feature  Start free, commit one file with your monitors and an alert channel, get your first alert. No credit card. Terminal npm install -g devhelm Start free — no credit card Book a demo Product Synthetics Webhook Testing Email Testing Build with DevHelm Developers Design partners Contact Company Security Privacy Terms DevHelm .io All systems operational © 2026 DevHelm.io Monitors are files. Review them like code. — DevHelm

